{{also|Trammel}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|tramail||net for catching fishes}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|tremaculum}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|æməl|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Whatever impedes activity, progress, or freedom, such as a net or shackle.
#* {{rfdate}} {{w|Jeffrey}}
#*: [They] disdain the trammels of any sordid contract.
#* {{quote-book|year=1898|year_published=1911|author=[[s:Author:William Graham Sumner|William Graham Sumner]]|title=[[s:War and Other Essays|War and Other Essays]]|chapter=[[s:The Conquest of the United States by Spain|The Conquest of the United States by Spain]]|publisher=Yale|page=[[s:Page:War and Other Essays.djvu/368|332]]|passage=The men who came here were able to throw off all the '''trammels''' of tradition and established doctrine.}}
# A [[fishing]] [[net]] that has large [[mesh]] at the edges and smaller mesh in the middle
# A kind of net for catching birds, fishes, or other prey.
#: {{rfquotek|Carew}}
# A set of [[ring]]s or other hanging devices, attached to a transverse bar suspended over a [[fire]], used to hang [[cooking pot]]s etc.
# A net for confining a woman's hair.
#* {{rfquotek|Spenser}}
# A kind of shackle used for regulating the motions of a horse and making him amble.
# {{lb|en|engineering}} An instrument for drawing ellipses, one part of which consists of a cross with two grooves at right angles to each other, the other being a beam carrying two pins (which slide in those grooves), and also the describing pencil.
# A [[beam compass]]

